Journal = {EXPERT SYSTEMS WITH APPLICATIONS},
Year = {2025},
Volume = {269},
Month = {APR 15},
Abstract = {The nonlinearity, nonsmoothness, and high-noise characteristics of
   complex signals pose challenges to accurate prediction infields such as
   geoscience and energy. This paper proposes a hybrid method combining a
   Differential Evolution-optimized Variational Mode Decomposition (VMD)
   with an improved Gated Recurrent Unit (GRU) model. The proposed method
   featured two main innovations: (1) In signal decomposition, the DE
   algorithm optimized VMD parameters, and intrinsic mode functions (IMFs)
   were reorganized through frequency feature extraction and K-means
   clustering, improving decomposition accuracy and robustness. (2) In
   prediction, a GRU network integrated with a Convolutional Neural Network
   (CNN) was augmented by an attention mechanism (CAGRU). The CNN layers
   extracted local features, while the attention mechanism highlighted
   critical information, enhancing the model's ability to handle noisy
   data. The proposed hybrid approach demonstrated significant performance
   enhancements: compared to the Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) model, the
   MAE was reduced by 75.34\%, the RMSE by 76.52\%, and the R2 increased
   from 0.1757 to 0.9543. Compared to the unoptimized VMD with CAGRU, the
   MAE and MSE were reduced by 25.45\% and 35.14\%. Compared to CAGRU with
   clustering and VMD, the MAE and MSE were reduced by 13.73\% and 21.32\%.
   Analyses of marine and terrestrial datasets demonstrated the method's
   superior accuracy and stability in predicting brightness temperature,
   achieving the highest precision among the methods compared and offering
   a novel approach for this field.},
